      "title": "Bally's discloses substantial doubt over going concern with $4.51bn of debt as Chicago dispute escalates",
      "text": "Bally's Corporation included a going-concern statement in its quarterly SEC filing, acknowledging \"substantial doubt about its ability to continue\" over the next twelve months - the same period in which it is due to open the delayed permanent Chicago casino in River West. The filing says the company \"is pursuing a number of financing alternatives intended to enhance its liquidity, including asset monetization, an equity sale and debt financing.\" Long-term debt including the current portion stood at $4.51bn as of 30 June against a market capitalisation near half a billion dollars, and 24/7 Wall St reported the shares fell 31% after the liquidity warning. The disclosure lands in the middle of an escalating fight with the City of Chicago: Bally's argues the city's legalisation of video gambling terminals breaches the 2022 host community agreement it says granted it a monopoly on virtual slot machines within city limits, after the City Council passed an alternate budget over Mayor Brandon Johnson's objections relying on $6.8m of new VGT revenue. On 8 August Bally's issued a \"reset\" notice pausing nearly all non-gaming amenities at the $1.7bn complex, putting the 34-storey hotel tower, events centre and restaurants on hold and idling roughly 200 of 1,500 site workers; 28 aldermen then signed a letter demanding a public hearing and accusing the company of using the VGT dispute as leverage. Bally's maintains the casino opening remains set for early next year and that it \"intends to fully honor its commitments under the Host Community Agreement.\" The group reported Q2 2026 revenue of $792.2m, up 20.5% year on year, spent $502m acquiring gaming licences in H1, and has signed a letter of intent with a potential equity investor for the $4bn Bally's Bronx project.",
