      "title": "Brazil says five million people are now barred from licensed betting accounts, about 10% of the registered base",
      "text": "Data consolidated to August 2026 by the Secretariat of Prizes and Betting (SPA) of Brazil's Ministry of Finance shows roughly 5 million Brazilians can no longer access licensed online betting platforms - about 10% of the 40 million active registered bettors recorded in the Betting Management System (Sigap). The blocks break down into three causes: around 3 million social-programme beneficiaries whose access was blocked by rule; 1.2 million bettors who voluntarily requested blocks via the SPA's centralised self-exclusion platform; and roughly 800,000 blocked following debt renegotiation, comprising 794,181 records tied to renegotiation programmes for defaulters and 33,123 linked to Fies student-loan agreements. The SPA has separately said 35% of those who self-excluded cited loss of control over gambling. Brazilian press coverage of the same dataset noted licensed operators handled roughly R$20bn in the first half of 2026, and the Ministry of Health says it has reinforced SUS provision for gambling addiction including psychological and psychiatric treatment and over 100,000 remote-care slots. For platform operators this is the first hard number on the addressable-base cost of Brazil's exclusion architecture, and it arrives while the SPA is consulting on a full rewrite of the authorisation regulation and Congress fields at least three prohibitionist bills.",
