      "title": "DraftKings self-certifies a combos contract, and the build-versus-route decision on prediction markets is now settled",
      "text": "DraftKings has self-certified a \"combos\" contract with the CFTC, positioning it to process parlay-style volume on its own exchange DKeX rather than routing users to Crypto.com as it does today. A combos contract settles on the joint outcome of two or more separately listed and certified DKeX contracts, is categorised as a swap, and would be titled \"Will all [outcomes] occur?\" on the exchange. The economics explain the urgency: combos already account for roughly 20% of Predictions consumer trading volume, more than half of Predictions customers have used them, and total Predictions trading volume grew to about $11bn annualised from $2.3bn during Q2. The filing followed CEO Jason Robins telling investors DraftKings plans to shift the \"vast majority\" of its sports volume onto its own exchange this autumn, and DKeX separately self-certified nine football contract categories around 12 August covering game winners, spreads, totals, player props and season awards for the NFL and college football. Read alongside Flutter's admission last week that the FanDuel Predicts rollout has run behind schedule, the pattern is clear: the two largest US operators have both concluded that renting a third-party exchange is a transitional arrangement, and both are absorbing order matching, contract listing and settlement into their own stacks. The platform implication for B2B vendors is that event-contract capability is becoming a core PAM requirement rather than a partnership - it needs to sit on the same wallet, the same customer record and the same RG and KYC rails as sportsbook and casino, with a contract-listing and settlement engine that a compliance team can configure without an engineering release.",
