      "title": "Brazil's 24-hour account freeze duty from 28 August pushes black-market enforcement into the payments stack",
      "text": "Under CMN Resolution No. 5.320, approved by Brazil's National Monetary Council on 25 June 2026 and taking effect on 28 August, banks and payment institutions must block all accounts of identified irregular fixed-odds betting operators within 24 hours of notification from the Secretariat of Prizes and Betting. The rule covers demand deposit, savings, prepaid payment and registration accounts; deposited funds become immediately unavailable and new transactions directed, directly or indirectly, to betting activity in those accounts are prohibited. In parallel the SPA, working with telecoms regulator Anatel, has blocked more than 56,000 domains linked to unauthorised operators, and in July the SPA and the Federal Revenue Service notified 37 fintechs suspected of processing payments for around 160 irregular betting houses, giving them until 28 August to sever those relationships or face joint liability and fines proportional to volumes moved. Brazil already restricts deposits to electronic bank transfers through Central Bank-authorised institutions, with credit cards, cash and cryptocurrencies barred. The practical consequence for licensed operators is that PSP counterparty risk is now a live operational concern rather than a procurement footnote: a payment partner that also serves grey-market brands can be frozen inside a day, taking legitimate deposit and withdrawal flow with it. That argues for redundant PSP routing per market, automated failover in the cashier, and a compliance view of each provider's wider client book before it is switched on.",
