    "summary": "Novig announced a responsible trading framework on 13 August, codifying standards directly into Chapter 14 of the Ludlow Exchange rulebook - creating affirmative obligations covering product design, marketing, monitoring of participant activity and promotion of responsible participation, including what it describes as the category's only nationwide 21+ age requirement.",
    "why": "Putting responsible-play obligations in the exchange rulebook rather than a policy document makes them enforceable by the venue against participants, which is the first credible answer to the charge that prediction markets have no equivalent of licensed operators' RG duties.",
    "expanded": "The package includes customer-set limits and requirements governing marketing and platform design, and Novig also announced work with the National Council on Problem Gambling on responsible trading initiatives. The framework follows Novig's relaunch as a federally regulated sports prediction market, live nationwide across 47 states from 4 August 2026. LSR framed the announcement against a Betterment study published the previous day showing more Gen Z individuals using investment funds at sportsbooks - which is the underlying behavioural concern: a product presented as trading rather than betting attracts users who have not self-selected into gambling safeguards. The design point worth extracting is the placement. A rulebook obligation binds through the venue's own disciplinary process and is visible to the CFTC, whereas an RG policy on a website is unenforceable against a participant. For any operator building an exchange, this establishes a template that regulators and courts will likely start treating as the reasonable standard, and a 21+ floor set nationally rather than per-state removes the argument that federal registration means the lowest common denominator on age.",
