    "meta": "SBC News | 14 Aug 2026",
    "summary": "Ukraine's State Online Monitoring System has moved into operation with the first 11 licensed operators connected and transmitting transaction data - bets, returns, winnings and other operations - to the state in close to real time, replacing reliance on periodic reporting, with Phase 2 targeting full land-based and online integration by December 2026.",
    "why": "Continuous transaction streaming is an architectural requirement, not a reporting feature: it needs a wager-granularity event bus that does not degrade in-play latency, schema versioning that survives regulator field changes, and a defined behaviour when the state endpoint is unavailable.",
    "expanded": "PlayCity, Ukraine's state agency for gambling and lottery oversight, has been headed by Hennadii Novikov since April 2025 and his tenure has been defined by SOMS development. It has issued 11 operator licences, three lottery licences and 213 gaming-equipment supplier licences - roughly 250 in its first year - alongside enforcement that has been described as aggressive, and it inherited significant gaps from predecessor regulator KRAIL. The design questions this raises are the ones that determine whether a market entry lands on time. Does the platform emit events at wager level or aggregate before transmission, and can the regulator's definition of a reportable event be changed by configuration? Is there replay and reconciliation for transmission gaps, with an auditable record of what was resent and when? And critically, what is the customer-facing behaviour if the state endpoint is down - does the bet get accepted and queued, or refused? Ukraine joins Romania, Greece, Colombia, Peru and the Philippines in expecting some flavour of near-live data, which is enough recurrence to justify treating regulator streaming as a first-class configurable capability rather than a per-market integration built by whichever squad owns the launch.",
