    "summary": "Brazilian operator Bet da Sorte has reinstated FIRST.bet as its sportsbook technology provider, covering trading, pricing and risk management across pre-match and in-play, while continuing to run its wider platform on Single Software - returning after a period trialling an alternative sportsbook supplier.",
    "why": "This is a clean example of the decoupled architecture Brazilian operators have converged on: PAM and wallet stay put while the sportsbook trading layer is swapped and, in this case, swapped back - which only works if the integration boundary was drawn properly in the first place.",
    "expanded": "Single Software is the shared iGaming technology stack used across Bet da Sorte's portfolio of Brazilian betting brands, so the sportsbook change was made without disturbing the account, wallet or compliance layer serving multiple brands under the same licence - the three-brands-per-licence structure Brazilian rules impose. The reversal is the interesting part: switching a trading supplier and then reverting inside a competitive cycle implies either that pricing and margin performance did not hold up, or that the operational cost of the change exceeded the expected gain. Either way it is evidence that sportsbook trading is now treated as a replaceable component rather than a platform commitment, and that operators are willing to bear switching cost to test it. The deal is part of a wider FIRST.bet regional push: it announced a partnership with Brazilian firm BetVip on 4 August, and Yogonet reported on 3 August that it had launched City Center Online's sportsbook in Argentina through a partnership with ONDISS. Brazil has been intensely competitive since regulation took effect on 1 January 2025, with local brands competing against Betano, bet365 and Superbet.",
